## ABSTRACT
Developing functional nanoagents for achieving the combination of microwave dynamic therapy (MDT) and thermal therapy (MTT) is highly desirable due to the advantages of improving the therapeutic effect of the tumor and minimizing the side effect. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as emerging porous materials, exhibit many intriguing properties for application in biomedicine. Herein, a new-style flexible Mn-doped zirconium metal-organic frameworks (MnZrMOF) nanocubes (NCs) with the average size of about $60\mathrm{nm}$ were prepared easily by one-pot hydrothermal method. Due to the strong inelastic collision of ions confined in a large number of micropores, the Mn-ZrMOF NCs were demonstrated to be an effective microwave-sensitive agent with a high thermal conversion efficiency up to $28.7\%$ , which is the highest one of the recently reported microwave-sensitive agents. This is the first report of determining the microwave thermal conversion efficiency, which can be used for evaluate, compare, and predict the microwave sensitivity of different microwave-sensitive agents. More importantly, such MnZrMOF NCs generate abundant reactive oxygen species (ROS) of hydroxyl radicals under microwave irradiation. As such, the Mn-ZrMOF NCs efficiently suppress the tumor cells growth in vivo and in vitro under mild microwave irradiation for the synergic effect of MTT and MDT.
This work paves the way for developing nanoagents that are responsive to microwave irradiation producing ROS and improving thermal effects to realize the non-invasive MTT and MDT treatment in clinics.
Searching for the efficient anti-tumor therapies is highly desired because of the unsatisfactory therapeutic efficiency of the traditional therapies like surgery, chemotherapy, and radiotherapy for killing the tumor cells partially.1-4 Recently, microwave thermal therapy (MTT) is a widely deemed as one promising therapy in clinic due to the simple operation, high efficiency, and small side effects. However, the recurrence rate after the treatment by traditional MTT is very high because of the limited ablation area of the tumor. Therefore, much effort has been devoted to improving the diffusion and the effective accumulation of the heat in the region of whole tumor by introducing of MW sensitive agents for MTT.5, 6 For example, we recently have developed sodium alginate-based microcapsules and ionic liquid $(\mathrm{IL})@Z^{\mathrm{{rO}_{2}}}$ nanoparticles as effective microwave-sensitive agents.7-10  However, the complicated tumor structures limits the efficiency of this strategy.11 As such we further developed a combined therapy by coupling MTT with chemotherapy based on the multifunctional nanoagents with both MW thermal and chemotherapeutic effects.12 Nevertheless, chemotherapy drug has the disadvantages of many side effects and drug resistance in the long term.13 We still need to find a better and safer synergy therapy to improve the therapeutic effect of MTT.
As known, dynamic therapy based on reactive oxygen species (ROS) can destroy the cancer cells and suppress the growth of tumor. Furthermore, ROS can be cleared out by the antioxidation system and thus shows no obvious side effect, as well as drug resistance. Typically, photodynamic therapy (PDT) has been applied as a site-specific targeting treatment for suppressing the growth of tumor in clinical application.14,15  A combination of MTT and MW dynamic therapy (MDT) with simultaneous production of thermal effect and ROS by microwave activation seems to be a more appealing synergy therapy to enhance antitumor efficacy. However, to the best of our knowledge, there is still no report of such a synergy therapy for treating tumor to date.
Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) formed by metal ions and organic bridging ligands are found great potential for biological applications due to their beneficial features of tunable size, shape, soft porous crystals and intrinsic biodegradability.16-18 As a rapidly growing class of nanocarriers with structure diversity, MOFs have been regarded as the prospective tools to address the challenge in drug delivery.19-23 For example, the octahedral pore diameter of UiO68-type MOF is $25.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , and is useful for DOX (dimension of $15.3{\times}11.9\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) loading. Thus, the loading and release of DOX in UiO-68-type MOF was successfully used for hepatoma (HepG2) therapy.24,25 Very recently, MOFs have been primarily studied as an ideal fourth generation of photosensitizer for PDT of tumor by using the photosensitizers porphyrin derivatives as the organic linker.26-28 Although a huge progress has been made on PTT or PDT by MOFs, other properties and applications of MOFs should be further explored.29
Enlighten by these, we report the first case of using of $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ doped Zr metal-organic frameworks nanocubes (Mn-ZrMOF NCs) for realizing synergic treatment of tumor by combining MTT and MDT. On one hand, the porous Mn-ZrMOF NCs with flexible structure are helpful for improving confined inelastic collision of ions, resulting in a high MW heating efficiency. On the hand, the in vitro experiments confirm that the Mn-ZrMOF NCs catalyze the production of hydroxyl radical (·OH) in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ under the MW irradiation. As such, the as-developed Mn-ZrMOF NCs not only greatly improve the conversion of microwave energy into heat but also promote ROS generation under MW irradiation. More importantly, the in vivo animal experimental studies demonstrate that the administration of Mn-ZrMOF NCs significantly inhibits the growth of tumor due to the combination of MTT and MDT. This work opens the door to treat tumor by a combination of MTT and MDT based on using the nanoagents that are effective for producing ROS and thermal effects under MW irradiation.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## Synthesis and Characterization of Mn-ZrMOF NCs
In this study, Mn-ZrMOF NCs were prepared by one-pot hydrothermal method in $10\mathrm{~h~}$ with the advantage of avoiding the intricate steps involved in post-modification. The use of polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), one kind of organic surfactant,30 is favorable to obtain uniform MnZrMOF NCs with excellent dispersibility. $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ -doping was performed to functionalize the ZrMOF.31 The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) in Figure 1a shows that the resultant MnZrMOF NCs prepared with PVP are uniform nanocubes with the average size of $60\pm5~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . This is further confirmed by the transmission electron microscope (TEM) observation (Figure 1b). The average hydrodynamic diameter of Mn-ZrMOF NCs was about $140\ \mathrm{nm}$ measured by dynamic light scattering in Fig. S1. Their polydispersity index was 0.0469, which demonstrated the good monodispersity, agreeing with the SEM results. Moreover, a large number of pores can be clearly observed in the Mn-ZrMOF NCs. The powder X-ray diffractometer (PXRD) patterns of both ZrMOF and Mn-ZrMOF NCs show sharp and strong diffraction peaks, suggesting their high crystallinity. All diffraction peaks of ZrMOF and Mn-ZrMOF NCs overlap well with simulated pattern of UiO-66 (Figure 1c),32 indicating that Mn-doping does not change the basic structure of UiO-66. $\Nu_{2}$ absorption test shows that the specific surface area of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs can reach $1132~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ due to the presence of abundant micropores with the average diameter centered at 4.33 and 5.82 Å. This is close to that of the reported UiO-66 $\cdot1264.3\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ ). Such a high specific surface area of the highly porous Mn-ZrMOF NCs are helpful for improving the thermal conversion efficiency under microwave irradiation due to the increased collision of ions in a confined area. Energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) pattern from SEM shows the presence of Zr, O, C, and Mn in the Mn-ZrMOF NCs (Figure S2), indicating that Mn was successfully doped in ZrMOF NCs. The quantitative analysis shows that the doping content of Mn in Mn-ZrMOF NCs is about $4.8\%$ by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES).
The modification of Mn-ZrMOF NCs with an amino-terminated polyethylene (PEG- $(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ ) was carried out to improve the biocompatibility of Mn-ZrMOF NCs.33 The FT-IR spectra showed that the characteristic stretching vibration of O-H at $3418~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and rocking vibration of N-H at $991~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs (Figure S3) were obviously increased after covalently coupling with PEG. Moreover, a new peak assigned to the stretching vibration of C-O at 1038 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appeared in the modified Mn-ZrMOF NCs, indicating that PEG- $(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ was successfully attached on the surface of Mn-ZrMOF NCs.
## MW heating experiment of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs in vitro
Selectively heating the cancerous tissue causing less elevated temperature of the healthy tissue is a very important factor for the hyperthermia treatment.34 As such, the microwave sensitivity of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs is firstly confirmed. As well known, the heating principle of microwave irritation is ascribed to the friction heat from the movable water or ions. Then, we examine that either the ions or water is more favorable to promote the MW heating effect of Mn-ZrMOF NCs.
As shown in Figure 2a, it can be clearly seen that no obvious change of temperature was observed in the pure water or water solution containing Mn-ZrMOF NCs after the MW irradiation for 5 min (1.8 W, 450 MHz). However, the temperature of the $0.9\%$ NaCl solution quickly rose to $42~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Furthermore, the temperature of the same NaCl solution with a further adding of Mn-ZrMOF NCs raised more significantly at a higher heating-up speed under the same condition (Figure S4), indicating that ions play an important role in the temperature rise under the MW irradiation. As shown in Figure S5, a higher temperature rise is achieved with an increased concentration of Mn-ZrMOF NCs. Remarkably, the temperature even reached $52~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with the presence of Mn-ZrMOF NCs at $20~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ . This indicates that Mn-ZrMOF NCs are more beneficial to convert the MW energy into the thermal energy. In addition, it is also found a close temperature rise of the NaCl solution with ZrMOF NCs and $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ ions at $20~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ under the similar MW irradiation (Figure 2a), suggesting that the porous structures caused the confinement effect. This is also agrees with our previous results.9 The Mn-ZrMOF and ZrMOF NCs can absorb and accommodate abundant ions due to the large specific surface area and abundant pores. In this case, strong inelastic collision would happen between the ions and the flexible framework of Mn-ZrMOF NCs under MW irradiation in such confined region, leading to generate lots of heat and the rapid increase of temperature. While for the $0.9\%$ NaCl solution without Mn-ZrMOF NCs, the collision motion among ions in the free space possessed a low frequency of collision, resulting in much less friction heat. Therefore, the Mn-ZrMOF NCs can act as MW-sensitive agent to promote the temperature rise under the MW irradiation.
## MW heating evaluation of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs in vitro
The MW thermal conversion efficiency is first use to evaluate the MW sensitivity (more details in Supporting Information). The process of MW heating can be divided into two stages. The stage I is the continued increase of temperature to a steady state under MW irradiation, and stage II refers to the decrease of temperature after removing the MW source. Figure 2c and shows the typical thermal curve of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs under MW irradiation. The MW thermal conversion efficiency (η) is calculated by the following formula.
$$
\eta=\frac{[\sum_{t=t_{0}}^{t=t_{n}}C_{0.9\%N a C l}\cdot m\cdot\Delta T+\mathcal{Q}_{l o s s}]}{\mathcal{Q}_{i n p u t}}
$$
where m is the mass of the $0.9\%$ NaCl solution and $\Delta\mathrm{T}$ is the temperature increase. $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{input}}$ is the energy from the MW source. $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{loss}}$ is the heat loss to the surroundings. $\boldsymbol{\upeta}$ is defined as MW thermal conversion efficiency.
The MW conversion efficiency of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs dispersed in saline solution is determined to be $28.7\%$ at $10~\mathrm{{mg~mL^{-1}}}$ (Figure 2b). To the best of our knowledge, this is the highest value among the previously reported values for the nanomaterials including $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2@}\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ with MW sensitivity.12, 35 Using this calculation method, we can evaluate, compare, and predict the microwave sensitivity of different microwave-sensitive agents.
It is well known that the effect of confined inelastic collision is positively correlated with the charge-mass ratio $\left(\mathrm{{q/m}}\right)$ of the ions. The ions with lower charge-mass ratio $\left(\mathrm{{q/m}}\right)$ show a lower kinetic energy and thus exhibit lower thermal energy conversion efficiency under MW radiation. To prove the confined inelastic collision between ions and Mn-ZrMOF NCs, the temperature elevation of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs dispersed in NaF, NaCl, NaBr, and NaI at $10.0~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL}^{-1}}}$ was monitored under MW radiation. The increased value for the temperature of the solution with MnZrMOF NCs is 11.15, 9.25, 7.20, and $4.25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ compared with that of the pure NaF, NaCl, NaBr and NaI solution at same concentration after MW irradiation, respectively (Figure 3). This is consistent with confined inelastic collision effect.
## MW-dynamic experiment of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs in vitro
Interestingly, it is found that Mn-ZrMOF NCs show peroxidase-like activity and thus can catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposition to produce ·OH under MW irradiation (Figure 4a). This is confirmed by detecting 3, 3′, 5, $5^{\prime}{\mathrm{.}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) based on that ·OH can oxidize TMB to oxidized TMB (oxTMB) with blue color, showing a characteristic absorption peak at 652 nm.36, 37 In order to avoid the thermal effect on the detection, the Mn-ZrMOF NCs dispersed in pure water were used for detecting ·OH since there is no obvious temperature rise under MW irradiation even for a long time. As shown in Figure 4a (insets), no color change was observed in the mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and TMB treated 5 min with MW irradiation at $1.8\mathrm{~W~}$ , indicating no decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with MW irradiation alone. While a slight blue color of oxTMB appeared in the mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and Mn-ZrMOF NCs after incubating for $5\mathrm{min}$ , suggesting the formation of a small amount of ·OH. However, a dark blue color was found in the mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and Mn-ZrMOF NCs as increasing the MW irradiation time, corresponding to an obvious increase of the maximum absorption at $652~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S7). This confirms that MW promoted peroxidase-like catalytic activity of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs. To the best of knowledge, such MnZrMOF NCs are the first MW-activated dynamic agent. Furthermore, it is found that increasing the concentration of Mn-ZrMOF NCs can greatly increase the absorbance intensity at $652~\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating the production of more hydroxyl radicals (Figure S8). When a higher concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was used as reaction substrate, more hydroxyl radicals were produced by adding a same amount of Mn-ZrMOF NCs under the same MW irradiation (Figure S9). The electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) measurement was used to further determine the hydroxyl radicals. A typical spin trapping agent of 5, $5^{\prime}.$ -dimethylpyrroline-N-oxide (DMPO) was used to trap hydroxyl radicals. As shown in Figure 4b, little ·OH was detected in the mixed solution of MnZrMOF NCs and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ without MW irradiation. After exposing with MW irradiation, the EPR signal with a 1:2:2:1 quartet characteristic was obviously enhanced, indicating that more ·OH was generated.
More interestingly, the color change and the absorption at $652~\mathrm{nm}$ were not observed with the ZrMOF NCs under the same MW irradiation (Figure 4a). Furthermore, the activation of ZrMOF by MW irradiation caused no enhancement of the EPR signal of ·OH. It can be concluded that
Mn-doping position in the ZrMOF NCs is functional sites for the production of ·OH.38, 39 This is also in line with the poor peroxidase-like catalytic activity of the pristine ZrMOF NCs. The MW irradiation can improve the peroxidase-like catalytic activity of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs was attributed to the following reasons. Firstly, the highly porous Mn-ZrMOF NCs have a high specific surface area and thus show more exposed active sites for catalysis in a confined space.40 Secondly, the transfer of electron between the Mn-ZrMOF NCs and substrates was efficiently promoted under MW activation, and then activated more $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from ground state into excited state, resulting in the production of abundant ·OH, as illustrated in Figure 4c and Figure S10.
## In vitro Biodegradability of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs
The biodegradability of Mn-ZrMOF NCs was preliminarily evaluated by incubation of MnZrMOF NCs at $1.0~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL}^{-1}}}$ in the artificial lysosomal fluid (ALF) for different times $(1\mathrm{~h},9\mathrm{~h~}$ , $12\mathrm{~h~}$ and $24\mathrm{h}$ ). The Mn-ZrMOF NCs was collected by centrifugation and washed with water for further characterization. The measurements of dynamic light scattering (DLS) showed that the Mn-ZrMOF NCs became larger with the poor dispersibility after incubation for 1 h (Table. 2s). As the incubation time in ALF $\mathrm{(pH}=4.5)$ ) increased, the Mn-ZrMOF NCs agglomerated obviously by TEM analysis. This is consistent with the formation of larger particles observed by DLS. After $^{9\mathrm{~h~}}$ of incubation, the morphology structure of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs obviously disintegrated (Figure S11). After incubation for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ , the Mn-ZrMOF NCs are significantly degraded, and little sedimentation was obtained even after centrifugation. Furthermore, PXRD test was performed to determine the crystallinity of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs after soaked in AFL for different times. As shown in Fig. S12, the PXRD patterns still show many peaks, especially two sharp peaks at $7.39^{\circ}$ and $8.53^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (111) and (002) planes 41 after soaking the Mn-ZrMOF NCs in AFL even for $^{9\mathrm{~h~}}$ , suggesting that they still have the crystalline structure in AFL. This is in consistence with the initial PXRD pattern before treating in AFL. However, the characteristic peaks of Mn-ZrMOF NCs disappeared in the PXRD pattern after soaking in AFL for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , indicating that the crystal structure of Mn-ZrMOF NCs was nearly damaged. These results indicate that the Mn-ZrMOF NCs can be biodegraded in vivo and excreted from the body gradually after performing the designated functions. The good degradability enables the MnZrMOF NCs as biosafe therapeutic nanoagents for potential clinical translation to achieve MTTMDT synergic therapy due to the catalytic production of ROS and thermal effects under MW irradiation.
## MTT Assay and Systematic Acute Toxicity of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs
Then the cytotoxic of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs were evaluated using HepG2 cells by MTT assay after the incubation for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The viability of HepG2 is more than $80\%$ at $63~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ (Figure S13), revealing a good biocompatibility of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs. The toxicity effect of MnZrMOF NCs on HepG2 cells with MW irradiation were also evaluated by a combination of calcein AM/propidium iodide (PI) staining and MTT assay. It is known that the temperature of thermal therapy is often about $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .42 During the experiment, near-infrared imaging equipment was used to monitor the temperature under MW irradiation. The results show that the temperature of the culture medium containing Mn-ZrMOF NCs at $62~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ rose up to $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after 2 min of MW irradiation at 0.9 W. As such, the HepG2 cells treated with irradiation at 0.9 W for 2 min and hot water bath at $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 min were set as experimental and control groups, respectively. Compared with over $80\%$ of cell viability in the control group of MW irradiation alone as well as hot water bath, the Mn-ZrMOF NCs plus MW treated group only shows $25\%$ of cell viability, revealing a high toxicity of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs to HepG2 cells under a mild MW irradiation. This is consistent with the observation by calcein AM/propidium iodide (PI) staining (Figure S14 and S15). Strong green fluorescence was observed in the control group and the group treated with Mn-ZrMOF NCs alone. However, almost all cells show red fluorescence. ICP-MS result showed that the Zr content in HepG2 cell increased as the time increased from $2\mathrm{h}$ to $24\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure S16). It is indicated that the efficient cellular uptake of Mn-ZrMOF NCs could exert synergic therapy of MTT and MDT under the MW irradiation to kill the tumor cells.
The evaluation of potential toxicity of MOF nanomaterials is required to understand the associated hazards in vivo. 43 The toxicity study was carried out by injecting the Mn-ZrMOF NCs at different concentrations of 120, 160, and $200~\mathrm{mg~kg^{-1}}$ . Although the statistical significances were observed in WBC and PLT from the mice treated with different concentration of MnZrMOF NCs in comparison with the control group, they are still in normal range (Figure 5). 44 The body weight and histomorphology examinations show no obvious abnormal results in the group with the Mn-ZrMOF NCs and the control group (Figure S17 and Figure 6). The visceral index of liver in the group injected with Mn-ZrMOF NCs at $200\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ is much higher than that of the control group (Figure S18), indicating that the Mn-ZrMOF NCs affected the liver at a higher concentration. These results indicated that the maximum tolerated dose of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs is about $160\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , demonstrating good biocompatibility of Mn-ZrMOF NCs.
## Underlying Mechanism of Cell Cytotoxicity Mediated by ROS
The 2, 7-dichlorofluorescin diacetate (DCFH-DA) dye can be oxidized to DCF by the presence of ROS in cells, showing green fluorescence when excited at $520~\mathrm{nm}$ . Therefore, DCFH-DA, as a ROS-index probe, was used to determine the production of hydroxyl radical with or without MW irradiation after the incubation of HepG2 cells and the Mn-ZrMOF NCs. The control groups without any treatment and MW only showed no fluorescence (Figure S14). As shown in Figure 7c, only weak green fluorescence was observed in Mn-ZrMOF NCs alone group, while bright green fluorescence can be seen in the HepG2 cells treated with Mn-ZrMOF NCs plus MW irradiation, implying that the Mn-ZrMOF NCs induce the production of ROS under MW irradiation and can be used as an alternative MW dynamic agent. These results demonstrate that Mn-ZrMOF NCs can be used for efficiently treating cancer cells by combining the MTT and MDT due to the excellent MW sensitivity and the production of hydroxyl radical.
## In Vivo Cancer Therapy
The distribution of Mn-ZrMOF NCs in the tumor is highly related to the therapy effect. Therefore, the targeting effect was evaluated by the analysis of $Z\mathbf{r}$ using the ICP-MS. At $6\textup{h}$ of post-administration, Zr element exhibited the highest content. As time increased, the content of Zr began to decrease due to being cleared out in Figure S19.45 Therefore, MW irradiation after post-injection $6\mathrm{{h}}$ was chosen. An obvious difference of temperature $\mathrm{\Delta}\Delta\mathrm{T}{=}10\ {^{\circ}}\mathrm{C}$ ) in tumor region was demonstrated between MW group and MW plus Mn-ZrMOF NCs group, by the infrared thermal imaging (Figure 7b), indicating the Mn-ZrMOF NCs possessed excellent microwave heating effect in vivo. Figure 7d compares the tumor size of mice in different groups after treating for 20 d. It can be seen that the administration with the Mn-ZrMOF NCs showed negligible influence on the tumor growth. Similarly, the tumor growth was slightly delayed under MW irradiation without sensitive agents. It is encouraging that the tumor of the mice subjected with the Mn-ZrMOF NCs and MW irradiation were almost destroyed. In order to confirm the synergistic effect of MTT and MDT by Mn-ZrMOF NCs, ZrMOF NCs was set as the control group of solo MTT because they showed no effects of MDT and had a similar MW conversion efficiency as that of Mn-ZrMOF NCs. Although the tumor region of mice injected with ZrMOF NCs can also reach $64.8\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ due to the effect of high MW sensitivity in Figure S20, tumor recurrence was observed at 5 day post-administration, indicating that tumor cells was not completed damaged by the heat alone in such a short time. It was clinically reported that irreversible cell damage can be induced when the temperature is over $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under the MW irradiation.46 A regular oval necrosis can be formed around the tumor after MW irradiation.
However, the recurrence and metastasis could happen around the tumor margin because the growth of the tumor is irregular.42 In comparison, the tumor disappeared after the mice injected with Mn-ZrMOF NCs. This is attributed to the following reasons. (1) A larger ablation area can be obtained with the assistance of Mn-ZrMOF NCs due to the better MW sensitivity. (2) The generation of •OH under the MW irradiation can further destroy the tumor cells that are in condition of sublethal and reversible damage in the margin of the tumor. As a result, the generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and the localized heating by Mn-ZrMOF NCs can enhance the treating efficiency of the tumor. Therefore, the Mn-ZrMOF NCs show good therapeutic efficacy due to the synergic effect of MDT and MTT. The survival rate of different groups also confirms that the group administrated with the Mn-ZrMOF NCs plus MW irradiation has the highest antitumor efficiency (Figure 7e), demonstrating the good therapeutic efficacy of Mn-ZrMOF NCs against H22-bearing mice by the synergic effect of MDT and MTT.
The tissues including tumor, spleen, liver, kidney, and lung of the mice were collected and carried out hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining after treating for 20 d. Most tumor cells are intact in the compared groups while those are totally disrupted in the group with Mn-ZrMOF NCs plus MW irradiation, However, compared to controls, no visible difference of form and lesion was observed in other organs of Mn-ZrMOF NCs plus MW (Figure 8), corresponding to the result of body weight (Figure S21).
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, for the first time, a biocompatible and biodegradable Mn-ZrMOF NCs prepared by hydrothermal method was demonstrated for realizing the efficient treatment of cancer through the synergy of thermal therapy and dynamic therapy under MW irradiation. The prominent therapeutic effect of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs is attributed to the unique composition and structure, including high specific surface, high porosity, and good dispersibility. Particularly, Mn-doping enables the ZrMOF NCs show peroxidase-like activity and thus can produce cytotoxic reactive oxygen species (·OH) to destroy cancer cells under MW irradiation. Besides, the excellent sensitivity greatly improves the MW thermal effect of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs with the highest microwave thermal conversion efficiency than the recently reported microwave-sensitive agents, which is determined by a new method for calculating the microwave thermal conversion efficiency of microwave-sensitive agents. Using this method, we can evaluate, compare, and predict the microwave sensitivity of different microwave-sensitive agents. The combination of MW thermal therapy and MW dynamic therapy enable the Mn-ZrMOF NCs as promising biosafe therapeutic nanoagents for the non-invasive treatment of deep tumor in vivo.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials and method: Zirconium (IV) chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4})}$ $(99.5\%)$ ) was obtained from Strem Chemicals, InSc. 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}B D C)}$ $(99\%)$ , N-hydroxysuccinimide (NHS) $(98\%)$ and  N-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)-N'-ethylcarbodimide hydrochloride (EDC) $(99\%)$ was obtained from J&K Scientific Co., Ltd. Manganese (II) chloride tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $99\%$ ) was provided by Shantou Xilong Chemical Co., Ltd. Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, $95\%$ ) was purchased from Shantou Xilong Chemical Co., Ltd. Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $99\%$ ), N-Ndimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ ), NaCl $(99\%)$ , and ethanol $(99.7\%)$ were purchased from Beijing Chemical Reagents Company. Perhydrol solution $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ $(30\%)$ was obtained from Tianjin Yongda Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -PEG- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{MW}=4000\ \mathrm{g\mol^{-1}},$ ) $(96\%)$ was provided by Beijing Kaizheng United Medicine Technology Co., Ltd. All of the reagents were used as received.
Synthesis of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs: Mn-ZrMOF NCs were prepared by a modified method referenced the previous literature.47 Typically, 13.4 mg $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ , 9.5 mg $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ and $7.5~\mathrm{mg}$ PVP were dissolved in $\ensuremath{12.45\mathrm{~g~}}$ DMF under vigorous stirring at room temperature. Then $2.3~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added in the above mixture to form a homogenous solution after sonication for a few minutes. ZrMOF NCs were prepared without doping $\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ based on the same formula. Subsequently, the resultant solution was transformed into Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave placed in oven for keeping at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling to the room temperature, the resultant solution was collected by centrifugation and repeatedly washed with DMF and ethanol for three times. Mn-ZrMOF power was finally obtained after vacuum drying at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In order to achieve the PEGylation of Mn-ZrMOF NCs, 10 mg Mn-ZrMOF NCs, 5 mg NHS, and 5 mg EDC were added to phosphate buffer saline $\mathrm{50.0~mL}$ , $\mathrm{pH}=5.5) $ ) under the magnetic stirring for $15~\mathrm{min}$ . The products were collected by centrifugation and washed with water. Then the asobtained sample was dissolved in $50.0\mathrm{mL}$ phosphate buffer saline $\mathrm{\Phi_{pH}}=7.4\mathrm{\AA}$ ) containing $10~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{PEG}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the as-prepared PEGylation of Mn-ZrMOF NCs was collected by centrifugation.
MW heating experiment of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs in vitro: Microwave heating experiment was carried out to analyze the microwave sensitivity properties of Mn-ZrMOF NCs. 1 mL MnZrMOF NCs and ZrMOF NCs dispersed in water or the saline solution at the different concentrations (20.0, 15.0, 10.0 and $5.0\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ) was added into a reaction vessel and irradiated at the out power of 1.8 W for 5 min. $1.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of the saline solution and water were used as the control group. An optical fiber probe was monitored the change of temperature, accompanying with infrared imaging. In order to study the effect of inelastic collision of ions confined in a large number of pores, the temperature change was recorded by dispersing the Mn-ZrMOF NCs (10.0 $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{mg}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{mL}}^{-1}$ ) in 1.0 mL NaF, NaCl, NaBr, and NaI aqueous solution $\left(0.154\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\right)$ ) were also tested under the same condition.
MW-dynamic experiment of the Mn-ZrMOF NCs in vitro: In order to avoid the heating effect,
Mn-ZrMOF NCs were dispersed in water because it shows no temperature increase under the microwave irradiation at 1.8 W. For detecting the reactive oxygen species (ROS), $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ TMB in DMSO, $100~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ Mn-ZrMOF NCs, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $(5~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ were dispersed in $2.0~\mathrm{mL}$ water, then the solution was added into a reaction vessel and irradiated for different time by microwave apparatus with the outpower of $1.8\mathrm{W}$ at the frequency of $450\mathrm{MHz}$ . Afterwards, the mixture was centrifuged and the supernatant liquor was analyzed by UV-vis spectroscopy (Jasco V-570 UV/vis/NIR).
ESR measurements: Based on the TMB test, EPR was used to determine the generation of hydroxyl radical (·OH) by 5,5'-dimethylpyrroline-N-oxide (DMPO). The EPR spectrum was collected by mixing Mn-ZrMOF NCs, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and DMPO under the MW irradiation. The blank ESR spectrum collected without irradiation.
Degradation in vitro: Degradation behavior of Mn-ZrMOF NCs in the artificial lysosomal fluid (ALF) was investigated in vitro. The simulated fluids were obtained according to previous reports.48 Then the Mn-ZrMOF NCs immersed in artificial lusosomal fluid (AFL) with the concentration of $1.0~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL}^{-1}}}$ was shook in a Heto Holten Denmark at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After incubating for different time, the samples were collected for TEM characterization and size measurement.
Cell experiments in vitro: To evaluate the therapy efficiency of Mn-ZrMOF NCs with a combination of MDT and MTT, cell toxicity was evaluated in vitro. Firstly, HepG2 cells were incubated with Mn-ZrMOF NCs at different concentrations for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , and then tested by methyl thiazolyl tetrazolium (MTT) method. Secondly, $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of HepG2 with the concentration of $1\times{10}^{4}$ was incubated with $0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of Mn-ZrMOF NCs at different concentrations in 6-well plate for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then, HepG2 cells uptake of Mn-ZrMOF NCs were irradiated after dispersing in a small plate with the output power of 0.9 W at 450 MHz for 2 min to control the temperature within $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Finally, the mixed solution of Mn-ZrMOF NCs and HepG2 cells was incubated for another $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to perform the evaluation of the cell toxicity by MTT assay and calcein-AM/ Propidium iodide (PI). In order to test the ROS production after MW irradiation, $1\times10^{4}$ HepG2 cells dispersed in DMEM were incubated with Mn-ZrMOF at the concentration of $6.25~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then $1.0\mathrm{mL}$ of the above mixture was microwave-irradiated for 2 min at 0.9 W to avoid the heating effect. $10\mathrm{\textmumol\LambdaL^{-1}}$ dichlorofluorescein diacetate (DCFH-DA) was added with culturing at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then the cells were washed by PBS 3 times by centrifugation before observing the fluorescence by a Nikon fluorescence microscope.
In vivo antitumor efficacy: ICR mice hearing H22 tumor cells were used to evaluate the antitumor efficacy complying with the Local Ethics Committee. The female ICR mice with the weight about $20{-}22\ \mathrm{g}$ purchased from Beijing Vital River Laboratory Animal Technology Co., Ltd were allowed to acclimate for 1 week. The H22 tumor cells $(2\times10^{6})$ were injected into the left hind of the mice. When the tumor grew to $200~\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ , the mice were divided five groups: (1) Control group, (2) MW alone, (3) Mn-ZrMOF NCs group, and (4) Mn-ZrMOF NCs and ZrMOF NCs plus MW $(\mathrm{n}{=}5)$ ). Mn-ZrMOF NCs dispersed in NaCl was injected via intravenous injection at $40~\mathrm{mg~kg^{-1}}$ in the Mn-ZrMOF NCs and Mn-ZrMOF NCs plus MW groups. The control and MW groups were administered an equivalent volume of NaCl solution. Post-injection $6\mathrm{~h~}$ , mice was anesthesia with pentobarbital sodium $(2\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , $0.2~\mathrm{mL}$ ) and the groups of MW and MnZrMOF NCs plus MW were conducted with MW irradiation at 1.8 W for 5 min. During the whole process, the temperature change in the tumor site was monitored by the real-time nearinfrared thermal imaging. The clinical manifestation was also observed carefully. The body weight and tumor size was recorded in detail during the whole experiment period. The experiment was terminated when the mice of control group were all dead. The tumor size was calculated according to the following formula: $V_{\mathrm{tumor}}=a b^{2}/2$ , where $a$ and $b$ are the length and width of tumor, respectively.
In vivo toxicity: In vivo toxicity evaluation was performed by the up-down methods. It is assumed that the maximum tolerance dose (MTD) of Mn-ZrMOF NCs is $175~\mathrm{mg~kg^{-1}}$ . The dose of $160~\mathrm{mg~kg^{-1}}$ was first injected to the mice. Within $24\mathrm{~h~}$ observation period, a slight passive behavior accompanied with weight loss and no other abnormal behaviors was observed at this dose. When the administration was increased to $200~\mathrm{mg~kg^{-1}}$ , the loss of appetite and passive behavior appeared in the mice. However, the mice returned normal after $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the mice exposed at $120~\mathrm{mg~kg^{-1}}$ showed no significant abnormal behavior during the whole experiment. The mice were sacrificed at 15 d. Blood from angular vein of the eye was collected for hematology analysis. The main organs of liver, spleen, lung and kidney separated from the necropsy were fixed in $10\%$ formaline. Histological examination was carried out by the following procedures: embedding in paraffin, sectioning, and staining with hematoxylin and eosin (HE). The tissue section was observed under optical microscope (Olympus X71).
Statistical analysis
The results are represented as means $\pm$ SD. One-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) tests were used to analyze the variance among the groups by SPSS 14.0. $P<0.05$ was presented as significant difference.
